## THE DECISIONS OF THIS LANE (each Tuesday's default as coordinator; each a Question at the stamp)
- **D1 (a, Q1): the line says what the code does; nothing is fixed.** The first half of the `SavedChanges` line ("is refused and the change is never written") stays: the gate measured it true (P8). Its "the caller sees the refusal" is true for a handler that does not catch; the new sentence names the catching handler. **The code paths that sentence covers (DERIVED, `SuiteDbContext.cs` at BASE):** the refusal is thrown from the change tracker's `StateChanged` event (`:206`) into the hook while a save runs; the entry stays `Modified`; the next save's pre-check `RefuseChangedAuditEntries()` runs first in both overrides, `SaveChanges(bool)` (`:95`) and `SaveChangesAsync(bool, CancellationToken)` (`:110`), before `base` saves anything. The cell runs the asynchronous path (the audit log's append, `Audit.cs:186`, `SaveChangesAsync`); the synchronous path is the same call, read from the code, not asserted. That EF Core routes its other `SaveChanges` overloads through these two is EF Core's behaviour, UNMEASURED here. A save interceptor's `SavedChanges` step that catches: the README says "read from the code and not asserted" (Q2).
- **D2 (b, Q3): the README reads "registration" as the call's own, and names the id across a `Remove`.** The gate's fix sketch read "registration" as the id in `:105`; that reading would make the sentence false (the old call's answer is disposed even when it is the object the id now holds), so the kit keeps "registration" as the holder's per-call entry and SAYS so in a new sentence, and the Limits line names the shape. **The code paths it covers (DERIVED, `CloudDeviceTokenHolder.cs` at BASE; the dispose sites printed by `grep -n -i 'Dispose()'`: `:159`, `:217`, `:240`, `:292`, `:330`, `:334`, and the public `Dispose()` at `:188`):** `Remove(id)` takes the entry out of the map (`:177`) and retires it (`:216` to `:218`); the next `GetAsync(id)` makes a new entry (`:73`); an old call's answer then reaches `Finish` on the OLD entry and is disposed because that entry is removed (`:328` to `:331`, in time) or `DisposeLate` on the OLD entry, which compares it with that entry's `Held` (null) and disposes it (`:236` to `:241`, after the bound). The cell covers both. No other dispose site is reached by an old call's answer (`:159` is `Invalidate`, `:217` `Retire`, `:292` an invalid answer, `:334` a renewal of the same entry; the gate's census RELAYED, re-read by the drafter).
- **D3: one cell each, each in a NEW file** (the commission's partition: "ONE new test file in each of `tests/Datasec.Suite.Data.Tests/` and the Universal Print registration test folder"). `tests/Datasec.Suite.Data.Tests/AuditSavedChangesLimitTests.cs` (class `AuditSavedChangesLimitTests`, `:9`) and `tests/Datasec.Suite.UniversalPrint.Tests/Registration/CloudDeviceTokenHolderLimitTests.cs` (class `CloudDeviceTokenHolderLimitTests`, `:10`), both picked up by the SDK's default compile items (no `.csproj` line: MEASURED, the build compiled them, totals +1 each). Each cell is one `[Fact]`; L01 runs two halves (in time, then late), each with its own holder, issuer and test clock; a mutant fails it at its first failing half.
- **D4: the cells' reds are their mutants', not `main`'s.** A cell that pins today's behaviour is GREEN at `main` by design (as kit B1936's catching-hook limit cell). Each is shown to be able to fail by two product mutants (THE CONTROLS), never committed. Every red is an assertion of the cell, never a compile error or a hang (the Universal Print reds are `Expect.Eventually`'s guard assertion, 10 s, MEASURED).
- **D5: ONE commit on a NEW branch from `main`.** No red state is committed; the mutants are run locally and kept in STATUS and `<lane>/logs/`.

